WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.860
A mouse taste cell can detect sweet, bitter or umami.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.300
But this cell has no conventional vesicular synapse.

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.620
So how does the news reach the nerve fibre?

4
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.260
Everything in this film is a mouse result.

5
00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.260
None of it is a human map.

6
00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:20.203
These are type II taste cells.

7
00:00:20.823 --> 00:00:23.563
They carry the receptors for sweet, bitter and umami.

8
00:00:24.263 --> 00:00:26.383
Those are G protein coupled receptors.

9
00:00:27.023 --> 00:00:30.803
When the cell is depolarized, it releases ATP through a channel.

10
00:00:31.483 --> 00:00:34.503
The nerve fibre beside it carries an ATP receptor.

11
00:00:35.617 --> 00:00:41.137
Taruno and colleagues found that CALHM1 is required for taste-evoked ATP release

12
00:00:41.137 --> 00:00:42.297
from mouse type II cells.

13
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:47.757
They used knockout mice, isolated cells, taste tissue, nerve recordings and

14
00:00:47.757 --> 00:00:50.597
behaviour. Without that channel, the release fails.

15
00:00:51.597 --> 00:00:54.977
Sour and salty recognition was mostly normal in the assays they tested.

16
00:00:55.597 --> 00:00:58.457
For sour, that fits release at a vesicular synapse.

17
00:00:58.457 --> 00:01:03.577
That does not mean that sodium signalling never uses CALHM channels.

18
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:05.900
A puzzle remained.

19
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.080
CALHM1 alone is slow.

20
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.400
In mouse N2a cells, recorded by whole-cell voltage clamp, it activated with a time

21
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.580
constant above 500 milliseconds.

22
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.560
Voltage clamp holds the membrane potential fixed while the channel currents are

23
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.560
recorded.

24
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.543
Ma and colleagues deleted Calhm3 in mice.

25
00:01:28.523 --> 00:01:31.243
Taste-evoked ATP release from taste buds was abolished.

26
00:01:31.923 --> 00:01:38.403
In the cultured cells, CALHM1 with CALHM3 activated in about 10 milliseconds.

27
00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:41.103
That is a speed-up of more than fifty-fold.

28
00:01:41.803 --> 00:01:48.723
The authors concluded that Calhm3 supplies the rapid gating that a CALHM1-only

29
00:01:48.723 --> 00:01:49.723
account lacked.

30
00:01:50.417 --> 00:01:51.837
Keep the limits in mind.

31
00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:54.937
The exact times depend on voltage and protocol.

32
00:01:55.557 --> 00:01:59.437
They are properties of cultured cells, not delays measured in a living animal.

33
00:02:00.157 --> 00:02:01.517
So here is the rule to keep.

34
00:02:02.137 --> 00:02:09.837
In mouse type II taste cells, ATP leaves through CALHM channels, and CALHM3 speeds

35
00:02:09.837 --> 00:02:10.337
the gating.
